---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';

const title = 'How to Fix Rock Sinking into Soil or Mud';
const metaDescription = 'Find out why gravel or landscape rock disappears into soil. Diagnose mud, buried stone, and drainage; rebuild a bed, path, or driveway with the right base and separator.';
const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Rock Sinking into Soil', url: '/how-to-fix-rock-sinking-into-soil' }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} metaDescription={metaDescription} canonicalPath="/how-to-fix-rock-sinking-into-soil">
  <Header />
  <main id="main-content">
    <PageHero
      title={title}
      subtitle="If stone keeps disappearing, lift a small patch before buying more. Mud between the stones calls for a firm base and separation; a clean bed buried under leaves or silt needs cleaning and runoff control. Driveway ruts need a stronger repair than a fresh surface layer."
      badge="Landscape rock and gravel repair"
      breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
      theme="utility"
    />

    <article class="repair-guide">
      <div class="container article-width">
        <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="On this page">
          <span>Go to</span>
          <a href="#diagnose">Find the cause</a>
          <a href="#bed-path">Bed or path</a>
          <a href="#driveway">Driveway</a>
          <a href="#materials">Choose a layer</a>
          <a href="#estimate">Estimate stone</a>
        </nav>

        <section id="diagnose" class="guide-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">Start with a shovel, not another load of rock</p>
          <h2>Is the rock sinking, being buried, or washing away?</h2>
          <p>Pull back roughly one square foot of the problem area. Look at the underside of the stone, the soil surface, and what happens after rain. These clues change the repair.</p>
          <div class="diagnosis-grid">
            <div class="diagnosis-card">
              <span class="card-mark">01 / Mixing</span>
              <h3>Mud comes up through the rock</h3>
              <p>Soft soil and stone are mixing, especially under feet or tires. Remove contaminated material, correct persistent wetness, and rebuild over a suitable separation layer.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="diagnosis-card">
              <span class="card-mark">02 / Burial</span>
              <h3>Stone is still there under dirt</h3>
              <p>Leaf litter, mulch, and windblown soil can cover a firm rock bed. Clean or sift reusable stone and keep new debris from collecting. Fabric underneath cannot stop buildup on top.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="diagnosis-card">
              <span class="card-mark">03 / Movement</span>
              <h3>Stone collects downhill</h3>
              <p>Runoff or traffic is moving the surface. Redirect water, contain the edge, and choose stone that suits the slope. See the <a href="/how-to-stop-gravel-washing-out-on-slope">gravel washout guide</a> for slope repairs.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <p class="field-note"><strong>Check the water first:</strong> A downspout, a low spot, or a blocked edge can keep the ground wet. Give runoff a lawful, stable route away from the house before covering the problem with stone.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="bed-path" class="guide-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">For decorative beds and footpaths</p>
          <h2>Rebuild the rock where soil and stone are mixing</h2>
          <figure class="layer-figure">
            <img src="/rock-sinking-repair-layers.svg" width="960" height="420" loading="lazy" alt="Cross-section comparing rock mixed into wet soil with a rebuilt bed: clean stone above a permeable separator on a prepared, draining soil surface." />
            <figcaption>A separator keeps fine soil and stone in their own layers; it cannot make a waterlogged site drain on its own.</figcaption>
          </figure>
          <ol class="repair-steps">
            <li>
              <h3>Recover usable stone</h3>
              <p>Rake or shovel it onto a tarp. If soil has filled the gaps, screen small batches and reuse the clean rock. Remove leaves, roots, and loose muddy material from the work area.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Prepare a firm, draining surface</h3>
              <p>Correct the source of standing water. Shape the exposed soil so water does not run toward the foundation or collect in the bed. Let saturated soil dry enough to work, then firm it without creating a bowl that traps water.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Separate only where it solves the problem</h3>
              <p>Where fine soil repeatedly enters the stone, lay a <strong>permeable geotextile intended for separation</strong> on smooth prepared soil. Follow its product guidance for overlaps, fastening, and cover. Thin weed-barrier film is not a substitute for a traffic-rated separator. Around plants you expect to divide or move, consider whether a buried sheet will complicate future work.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Replace stone to your chosen finished depth</h3>
              <p>Spread the cleaned rock evenly and add only the amount needed to restore the surface. Keep soil and mulch from spilling over the edge. For a walking path, use a compactable angular base where needed before the visible stone; loose rounded pebbles alone make an unstable walking surface.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="field-note">If large edging stones appear to sink while the surrounding bed rises, dig one out and compare soil levels. Decomposed mulch and deposited soil may be burying it; clean and reset the edge instead of rebuilding the whole bed.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="driveway" class="guide-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">For wheel loads and recurring ruts</p>
          <h2>A muddy driveway needs drainage and a sound base</h2>
          <p>Repeatedly topping up a rut can waste stone when tires are pressing aggregate into wet fine soil or pumping mud into the base. First restore a surface shape that sheds water and an outlet that can carry it away. Then excavate the soft pocket or failing section to a firm working surface. A contractor can size the base for the soil, traffic, and climate; there is no universal three-inch fix for every muddy driveway.</p>
          <div class="driveway-panel">
            <div>
              <h3>Typical rebuild sequence</h3>
              <ol>
                <li>Remove loose, mud-contaminated aggregate and soft organic material.</li>
                <li>Repair the water path and prepare the subgrade without churning wet soil into more mud.</li>
                <li>Where specified, place road-grade separation geotextile; add geogrid only when the base design calls for reinforcement.</li>
                <li>Place suitable angular aggregate in compacted lifts, then finish the surface so water can leave it.</li>
              </ol>
            </div>
            <aside>
              <h3>Geotextile ≠ geogrid</h3>
              <p>Geotextile can keep fine soil from mixing into the aggregate. Geogrid helps restrain aggregate laterally, but its open apertures do not filter fine soil by themselves. A weak, wet subgrade may need both, plus enough aggregate cover. Product choice and thickness depend on site conditions.</p>
            </aside>
          </div>
          <p class="field-note">A spring, persistent ponding, or ruts that return after a base rebuild deserve a site assessment. A perforated drain helps only when it has the right location, filter detail, and permitted outlet.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="materials" class="guide-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">Make the layers match the job</p>
          <h2>What belongs beneath the visible rock?</h2>
          <div class="table-scroll">
            <table>
              <caption>Choose by failure mode and traffic, not by fabric weight alone</caption>
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">Area</th><th scope="col">Useful layer or action</th><th scope="col">What it will not solve</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Decorative bed</th><td>Clean the stone and correct runoff; use a permeable separator if soil keeps mixing into it.</td><td>Leaves and dust accumulating above the stone.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Walking path</th><td>Firm subgrade, suitable separator where needed, and a compactable base below the finish stone.</td><td>A soft path caused by standing water or a buried organic layer.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Driveway</th><td>Drainage, road-grade separator where needed, and an aggregate base sized for vehicle loads.</td><td>Ongoing pumping if the subgrade and water source remain untreated.</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Very weak ground</th><td>Site-specific stabilization, sometimes geogrid with a separate filtration layer.</td><td>Waterlogged soil with nowhere for water to go.</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <p>Don't buy fabric from an ounce-per-square-yard number alone. For a loaded base, the supplier or designer should check separation and filtration needs, survivability during placement, and the aggregate placed above it. FHWA guidance distinguishes these functions from reinforcement.</p>
        </section>

        <section id="estimate" class="guide-section estimate-section">
          <p class="eyebrow">Order after the repair depth is decided</p>
          <h2>Estimate the stone to put back</h2>
          <p>Measure the repaired <strong>length × width</strong> and the finished depth of each layer. For a rectangular area, cubic yards = length (ft) × width (ft) × depth (in) ÷ 324. Reused clean rock reduces the amount you need to buy.</p>
          <div class="estimate-example"><strong>Example:</strong> A 10 × 12 ft bed restored to 2 in of stone needs <strong>0.74 cubic yards</strong> before any project-specific ordering allowance: 10 × 12 × 2 ÷ 324 = 0.74. Ask the supplier for the delivered material's bulk density if you order by tons.</div>
          <div class="tool-grid">
            <a href="/rock-calculator"><strong>Rock calculator <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></strong><span>Size a decorative surface layer by area and depth.</span></a>
            <a href="/crusher-run-calculator"><strong>Crusher run calculator <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></strong><span>Enter the target compacted depth and adjust its compaction allowance for a base layer.</span></a>
            <a href="/drain-rock-calculator"><strong>Drain rock calculator <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></strong><span>Estimate clean aggregate for a separately designed drainage trench.</span></a>
          </div>
          <p class="calc-caveat">These tools estimate material volume. They cannot diagnose the soil, size a road base, or determine a drain outlet.</p>
        </section>

        <section class="guide-section related-section" aria-labelledby="related-heading">
          <h2 id="related-heading">Keep the repaired surface working</h2>
          <div class="related-grid">
            <a href="/how-to-stop-gravel-washing-out-on-slope"><strong>Gravel moving downhill?</strong><span>Control runoff and stabilize the slope.</span></a>
            <a href="/how-to-stop-weeds-in-landscape-rock"><strong>Weeds in the recovered stone?</strong><span>Remove the soil and organic matter that feeds new growth.</span></a>
            <a href="/rock-depth-guide"><strong>Unsure about finished depth?</strong><span>Compare depth by material size and application.</span></a>
          </div>
        </section>

        <aside class="source-note" aria-label="Research sources">
          <h2>Field references</h2>
          <p>This guide applies the separation, reinforcement, and drainage distinctions in the <a href="https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/engineering/geotech/pubs/05037/07d.cfm">FHWA geosynthetics guidance</a> and its <a href="https://www.fhwa.dot.gov/construction/pubs/ots15002.pdf">gravel roads construction guide</a>. The <a href="https://www.ncagr.gov/divisions/nc-forest-service/wq0214/download?attachment=">North Carolina Forest Service road guide</a> describes geotextile and stone choices for soft, wet areas. Final driveway details depend on the site.</p>
        </aside>
      </div>
    </article>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .repair-guide { padding: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 4rem) 0 5rem; }
  .article-width { max-width: 1100px; }
  .jump-nav { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; align-items: center; gap: .55rem; padding: 1rem 1.2rem; margin-bottom: 3rem; border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); background: var(--surface-card); }
  .jump-nav span { font-size: .78rem; font-weight: 800; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .08em; color: var(--text-muted); margin-right: .3rem; }
  .jump-nav a { color: var(--text-primary); text-decoration: none; padding: .35rem .65rem; border-radius: 999px; background: rgba(245, 158, 11, .1); font-size: .86rem; }
  .jump-nav a:hover, .jump-nav a:focus-visible { background: rgba(245, 158, 11, .24); }
  .guide-section { margin: 0 0 4.5rem; scroll-margin-top: 5rem; }
  .eyebrow { margin: 0 0 .5rem; color: var(--accent-gold); text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .12em; font-size: .76rem; font-weight: 800; }
  .guide-section h2 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3vw, 2.3rem); line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 1.2rem; letter-spacing: -.025em; }
  .guide-section h3 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1.1rem; line-height: 1.3; margin: 0 0 .55rem; }
  .guide-section p, .guide-section li { color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.72; }
  .guide-section > p:not(.eyebrow), .guide-section > .field-note { max-width: 800px; }
  .guide-section a { color: var(--accent-gold); }
  .diagnosis-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.6rem 0; }
  .diagnosis-card { padding: 1.35rem; border-radius: var(--radius-lg); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); background: var(--surface-card); }
  .card-mark { display: block; color: var(--accent-gold); font-size: .72rem; font-weight: 800; text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: .1em; margin-bottom: .8rem; }
  .diagnosis-card p { font-size: .93rem; margin: 0; }
  .field-note { background: rgba(245, 158, 11, .1); border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-gold); border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0; padding: .9rem 1.2rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 0; }
  .field-note strong { color: var(--text-primary); }
  .layer-figure { margin: 1.8rem 0 2rem; border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); overflow: hidden; background: #f2efe8; }
  .layer-figure img { display: block; width: 100%; height: auto; }
  .layer-figure figcaption { padding: .8rem 1rem; color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: .84rem; background: var(--surface-card); }
  .repair-steps { counter-reset: step; list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; }
  .repair-steps li { counter-increment: step; position: relative; border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); background: var(--surface-card); padding: 1.25rem 1.35rem 1.25rem 4rem; }
  .repair-steps li::before { content: counter(step, decimal-leading-zero); position: absolute; left: 1.2rem; top: 1.2rem; color: var(--accent-gold); font-weight: 800; }
  .repair-steps p { margin: 0; font-size: .94rem; }
  .driveway-panel { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1.4fr 1fr; gap: 1rem; margin-top: 1.4rem; }
  .driveway-panel > div, .driveway-panel aside { padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: var(--radius-lg); border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); background: var(--surface-card); }
  .driveway-panel ol { margin: .5rem 0 0; padding-left: 1.25rem; }
  .driveway-panel li { padding-left: .2rem; margin-bottom: .5rem; }
  .driveway-panel aside { border-color: rgba(245, 158, 11, .4); }
  .driveway-panel aside p { margin: 0; }
  .table-scroll { overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; min-width: 640px; background: var(--surface-card); }
  caption { text-align: left; padding: 1rem 1.2rem; color: var(--text-muted); font-size: .87rem; }
  th, td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: 1rem 1.2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); color: var(--text-secondary); line-height: 1.5; }
  thead th, tbody th { color: var(--text-primary); font-weight: 700; }
  thead th { background: rgba(245, 158, 11, .08); }
  .estimate-example { max-width: 800px; padding: 1.1rem 1.3rem; background: rgba(245, 158, 11, .1); border-radius: var(--radius-md); color: var(--text-primary); line-height: 1.6; margin: 1.3rem 0; }
  .tool-grid, .related-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .tool-grid a, .related-grid a { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .5rem; padding: 1.2rem; border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); border-radius: var(--radius-lg); background: var(--surface-card); text-decoration: none; color: var(--text-primary); }
  .tool-grid a:hover, .related-grid a:hover { border-color: var(--accent-gold); }
  .tool-grid span, .related-grid span { color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: .89rem; line-height: 1.5; }
  .calc-caveat { font-size: .88rem; }
  .related-section { padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); }
  .source-note { border-top: 1px solid var(--border-subtle); padding-top: 1.2rem; max-width: 800px; }
  .source-note h2 { color: var(--text-primary); font-size: 1rem; margin: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .source-note p { color: var(--text-muted); font-size: .83rem; line-height: 1.65; }
  .source-note a { color: var(--text-secondary); }
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .diagnosis-grid, .tool-grid, .related-grid, .repair-steps, .driveway-panel { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } .guide-section { margin-bottom: 3.5rem; } }
</style>
